{
  "dom_id": "step-1238",
  "name": "Relative Extrema",
  "problem": "Recall that we can use the first derivative test to identify the relative extrema of a continuous function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *positive* to *negative* around $x=a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/9912cc68ed61884957907c360f172fe37582827b67e0b9873795ae475dc1e86c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *negative* to *positive* around $x=a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/b8da20322f6797554f2c52642fd313df78ce1a7a67f54e3c524af446398ce515.png)\n\nSo, to determine the relative extrema of a function $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to the locations where $f'(x)$ changes sign.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
